   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to implementing a statewide\nelectronic tracking system for evidence collection kits used to collect and\npreserve evidence of a sexual assault or other sex offence that are submitted\nto the custody of law enforcement; and to amend a chapter of the laws of 2022\namending the public health law relating to implementing a statewide electronic\ntracking system for evidence collection kits used to collect and preserve\nevidence of a sexual assault or other sex offense, as proposed in legislative\nbills numbers S. 7867-A and A. 9596-A, in relation to the effectiveness\nthereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S2226",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2226-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2805-i, Pub Health L; amd §2, Chap of 2022 (as proposed in S.7867-A & A.9596-A)",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S2226\n\nSPONSOR: WEBB\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to implementing a\nstatewide electronic tracking system for evidence collection kits used\nto collect and preserve evidence of a sexual assault or other sex\noffence that are submitted to the custody of law enforcement; and to\namend a chapter of the laws of 2022 amending the public health law\nrelating to implementing a statewide electronic tracking system for\nevidence collection kits used to collect and preserve evidence of a\nsexual assault or other sex offense, as proposed in legislative bills\nnumbers S. 7867-A and A. 9596-A, in relation to the effectiveness there-\nof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2022,\nc.  , relating to the development and implementation of a statewide\nelectronic tracking system for sexual offense evidence collection kits\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe underlying legislation Requires the development and implementation\nof a statewide electronic tracking system for sexual offense evidence\ncollection kits to allow survivors to track or receive updates regarding\nthe status and location of their kit through the criminal justice proc-\ness; initial collection performed at a healthcare facility, receipt and\nanalysis by a crime laboratory, and storage and destruction following\nanalysis.\n\nChanges included in this Chapter narrow the applicability to evidence\nkits that are sent to law enforcement, and not those sent to storage\nwhich are processed under a separate system. Changes also adds prosecu-\ntors as an entity that must comply with DCJS protocols, rules and guide-\nlines relating to kits \"taken custody\" of and removes medical providers\nas an entity subject to these protocols since there are existing guide-\nlines for the medical community.Employees of the long-term sexual\noffense evidence storage facility are also as an entity who may update\nand track the status of location of kits sent to law enforcement.\n\nFinally, changes provided within this Chapter pushes out the date the\nsystem must be fully operational from 1 January 2024 to 1 January 2025.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\n§ 2805-i of the Public Health Law\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation is a negotiated change to the underlying Chapter.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter     of the Laws of 2022. S.7867-A and\nA.9596-A both passed the Senate and Assembly in June.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and § 1 of its underlying Chapter\nof the Laws of 2022, relating to the development and implementation of a\nstatewide electronic tracking system for sexual offense evidence\ncollection kits, as proposed in legislative bill numbers S.7867-A and\nA.9596- A shall take effect 180 days after being signed into law.\n",
